WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.770
The chances of Mark Ruffalo getting a solo
Hulk movie seem like they're slim-to-none,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.980
but the Hulk's been one of the most visible
superhero movie stars for decades.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.040
Here's a look back at all the Hulk movies,
ranked from worst to best.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.759
The last of three Incredible Hulk revival
TV movies was The Death of the Incredible

00:00:18.759 --> 00:00:23.490
Hulk, but despite the title and its status
as the final chapter, Banner's death wasn't

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.560
meant to be permanent.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.380
There was meant to be a follow-up called The
Revenge of the Incredible Hulk, but it never

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.750
happened, possibly due to star Bill Bixby's
failing health, but more likely because Death

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.700
of the Incredible Hulk got abysmally low ratings.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.240
The good news is that this meant very few
people got to see the moronic manner of the

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:41.840
Hulk's "death."

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.480
He falls from an airplane.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.320
Seriously, that's it.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.830
If you're a fan of both the comics and the
show, you learned a long time ago to accept

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.360
that TV's Hulk wasn't even close to the comics
version in terms of power.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.440
Still, the Hulk dying from a long fall would
be like Logan ending with Wolverine dying

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.440
from the flu.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.400
Part of the goal of the late '80s Incredible
Hulk revival was to use a familiar face to

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.580
introduce some of the other Marvel franchises
to audiences.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:12.940
That's why Trial of the Incredible Hulk was
the debut of the first live-action Matt Murdock,

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:17.760
a.k.a. Daredevil, who represents Banner after
he witnesses a crime linked to the Kingpin.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.950
While Daredevil fans likely wouldn't put Trial
of the Incredible Hulk any higher on a list

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.369
than Hulk fans would, the movie gives way
more attention to the Man Without Fear than

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:28.380
the Green Goliath.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.399
While he makes a ruckus earlier in the movie,
Banner's gigantic alter-ego doesn't even get

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.940
close to the climactic action during the final
fight.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.319
Instead, pretty much everything is in the
hands of Daredevil's badly choreographed martial

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:40.820
arts.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:45.580
Ang Lee's Hulk is not well-loved, but maybe
it deserves a break.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:49.640
At the very least, it was trying to be more
than just another popcorn action flick.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.740
The script isn't great and most of the performances
are wooden.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:57.000
The story is often boring, with Lee waiting
a long time to introduce the Hulk and waiting

00:01:57.009 --> 00:02:01.580
even longer for the film's only impressive
action scene: Hulk's battle in the desert

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:02.400
with the military.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.509
The effects are inconsistent, with some shots
coming off as stunning, and others are embarrassingly

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:08.400
cartoonish.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.160
That said, there's a lot of mythic imagery.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.439
Lee connects the Hulk with nature, which is
why he works in moments with the Hulk mesmerized

00:02:15.439 --> 00:02:19.459
by certain rock formations, and why it almost
makes sense that one of the most memorable

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.720
fights in the film comes when the Hulk takes
on a bunch of gamma-powered Hulk Dogs.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:24.220
Almost.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.680
Lee tried to make something unique and entertaining.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.080
He thoroughly fails at the latter, but you
have to admit that he went for it.

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:36.040
In 1988, American network television got its
first new installment of David Banner's lonely

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:40.720
saga since 1982 in the made-for-TV movie The
Incredible Hulk Returns.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.060
Along with Bill Bixby and Lou Ferrigno, Returns
stars Eric Allan Kramer as Thor and Steve

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.800
Levitt as the thunder god's classic alter-ego,
Donald Blake.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.160
It's definitely pretty far from perfect, but
of the three TV movies, Returns is by far

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:55.960
the most bearable.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.860
Unlike Death, the Hulk doesn't die in a ridiculous
way, and unlike Trial, the guest superhero

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.960
actually adds to the plot instead of completely
stealing the show.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:09.400
Hulk and Thor have their compulsory battle
in a lab, almost 25 years before Chris Hemsworth

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.629
and Mark Ruffalo would tussle on the deck
of a Helicarrier or duke it out in an outer

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:15.360
space gladiatorial arena.

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:19.760
Admittedly, their eventual team-up later in
the film feels way more like a fight from

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.230
an episode of The A-Team than an epic clash
of strength between the strongest Avengers,

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.379
but the fight is still absolutely worth watching.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.380
If nothing else, you can see how far we've
come since 1988.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.200
If this were a different kind of ranked movie
list, there's a really good chance Avengers:

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.519
Infinity War would make it a lot closer to
the top, but as a Hulk movie, it doesn't have

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:39.320
much to offer.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:44.220
After that first opening scene on the refugee
ship, the Hulk pretty much disappears.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:48.920
We see a few flashes of green when Bruce Banner
tries and fails to get the "Other Guy" to

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.440
come out, and it even has a couple of good
Banner moments.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:52.960
"Aha!

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.100
You guys are so screwed now!"

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:59.220
That's about it, though, and even worse, the
Hulk totally gets Worfed.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:03.860
If you're not familiar with "The Worf Effect,"
the name refers to how often the Enterprise's

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.700
Klingon security chief gets beat up on Star
Trek: The Next Generation.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:12.319
It's an easy trope; the bad guy beats up the
biggest, baddest good guy early in the story

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:14.260
so the audience knows he's a threat.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:18.120
The Hulk getting trounced and then nearly
vanishing would each be bad enough on their

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.500
own, but together?

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:23.780
We eventually got some good character development
out of it, but for Hulk fans, it adds some

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:26.120
surprising disappointment to Infinity War.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.000
"Hulk, we got a lot to figure out, pal."

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:35.600
Incredible Hulk is probably not on the top
of anyone's list of MCU films, but the truth

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:37.900
is, it's probably a whole lot better than
you remember.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.660
At the very least, it's not bad.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:44.620
For one thing, it introduces the green guy
much sooner than its 2003 predecessor.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.240
For another, it gives us a lot more action
in the form of one classic Hulk villain, the

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.740
Abomination, and the foundation for the emergence
of another in the form of Dr. Samuel Sterns,

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:59.040
a name Hulk fans would recognize belonging
to the mastermind villain the Leader.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.300
It even has Stan Lee turning into a Hulk,
and even if that happens offscreen, it's definitely

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.120
fun to think about.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:09.660
Plus, it stands to reason that Ed Norton could
have been the perfect Bruce Banner.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.030
He built his career playing characters who
were meek pushovers one second and intimidating

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:15.240
bullies the next.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:19.920
Unfortunately, his talents aren't really used
well in what amounts to a formulaic action

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.660
flick with no real glimpse of who Bruce Banner
is.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:27.520
The CGI rendering of the Hulk is definitely
more consistent than what we saw in Ang Lee's

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:32.520
Hulk, and the monster's bellowing roar is
a huge improvement, but watching Incredible

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:36.820
Hulk now, the effects look primitive compared
to what we see in everything that comes after.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.700
For a Hulk fan, Age of Ultron was a tough
pill to swallow.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.500
For the sake of the plot, Iron Man needs to
win their mid-movie tussle, and as cool as

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.830
it is to see the Hulkbuster armor in action,
it raises the question of why they even need

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:53.900
the Hulk around if Tony Stark can just build
something stronger whenever he wants.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.720
It does have some bright spots, though.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:00.300
Regardless of who wins, the Hulk/Iron Man
battle is wonderfully executed, and in the

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:05.900
final conflict, Hulk's probably the only Avenger
to successfully use his teeth to trash robots,

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.240
and takes out Ultron in one shot… twice.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:11.800
There are also some wonderful character moments
for Hulk in Age of Ultron.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:16.199
Whether you like the romance with Black Widow
or not, it reveals an interesting side of

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:17.500
Banner we haven't seen before.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.200
While most of this list is for live-action
flicks, 2010's Planet Hulk is a worthy exception.

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:28.020
Based on the comic storyline of the same name,
Planet Hulk is about the Green Goliath being

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:33.440
exiled by Earth's other heroes to the planet
Sakaar, where he becomes a gladiator and eventually

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.980
leads a revolt against the tyrannical Red
King.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.920
Planet Hulk was about as faithful to its source
material as a comic book movie could get.

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:44.720
Stuff is left out for time, with the final
battle between Hulk and the Red King playing

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.180
out a little differently and the Hulk's major
gladiatorial battle is against Thor's spacefaring

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.940
buddy Beta Ray Bill instead of the Silver
Surfer.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:56.360
The biggest change, though, is that it stops
short of the cataclysmic explosion that the

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:57.360
comic story ends with.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:01.880
In the comics, that acted as a prologue to
Hulk returning to Earth for payback in the

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:02.560
next storyline.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:06.440
That last omission, however, is one of the
best things about Planet Hulk, because it

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.020
gives us something we rarely see in a Hulk
movie: a happy ending.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:15.640
Looking at 2017's Thor: Ragnarok through the
eyes of a Hulk fan can feel very bittersweet.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:20.729
On one hand, it's the only live-action adaptation
of Planet Hulk fans are likely to get, but

00:07:20.729 --> 00:07:24.360
on the other, it's tough to know that the
closest we'll get to that story is a film

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:26.610
dedicated to one of the character's biggest
rivals.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:28.400
"So how long have you been like that?"

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:30.000
"Like what?"

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:31.600
"Like this.

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:32.860
Big and green.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:33.960
Stupid."

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.020
"Hulk always Hulk."

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:41.500
The Hulk's dialogue perfectly captures what
any old school Marvel fan should remember

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:46.400
of the Savage Hulk's monosyllabic speech,
but in the arena, he winds up having to unwittingly

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:47.580
cheat to win the fight.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:49.430
"Voice activation required."

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:50.479
"Banner."

00:07:50.479 --> 00:07:52.419
"Welcome, Strongest Avenger."

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:53.000
"Uh, what?"

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.880
Despite the contradictions, what keeps Ragnarok
so close to the top of this list is the banter

00:07:57.889 --> 00:08:02.120
between Hulk and his best frenemy Thor and
all the comedic moments that go along with

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:06.220
it, especially the Hulk's dogged determination
to take down Surtur at the end.

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:10.060
As a Hulk film it's far from perfect, but
it's still pretty great.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:15.860
Infinity War gave us a lot of Banner and very
little Hulk, but 2019's Avengers: Endgame

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:20.389
rewards fans with a lot of both by showing
us a Banner who stopped treating the Hulk

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:24.560
"like a disease" and used his genius to merge
his mind with the Other Guy's body.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.560
We get to enjoy his embarrassment at the earlier
Hulk's caveman persona, and once the heroes

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:34.340
gather the Infinity Stones, only Hulk has
the power to undo Thanos' purge and survive

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:34.840
the ordeal.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:39.320
We even get a tribute to his classic Secret
Wars feat of holding a mountain range at bay.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:43.520
Unfortunately, the Hulk never gets another
shot at Thanos; in fact, he doesn't get much

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:45.220
time in the final battle at all.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:49.500
It's a pretty crowded scene already, sure,
but after their first battle ended in such

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:53.220
a crushing defeat, you really want to see
as much smashing as possible.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:59.200
It's a little ironic that 2012's Avengers
is above and beyond all other Hulk movies.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:03.940
Not only is his name not even in the title,
but he actually isn't in it all that much.

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:08.120
His screen time was limited to the film's
two big action sequences, but that was enough

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:11.850
for him to completely steal the show, and
provide him with a line that's every bit as

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.570
iconic as the classic "don't make me angry."

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:16.180
"That's my secret, Cap.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:19.920
I'm always angry."

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:24.980
That moment marks the final cohesion of the
team, and made him the movie's most crowd-pleasing

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:25.480
star.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:30.380
His hilarious sucker punch on Thor in Grand
Central Station, the epic thrashing of Loki

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:34.390
in Stark Tower, and the unique way that he
helps to bring Tony Stark back to life are

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:36.460
all pure gamma-powered gold.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:40.839
For the first time, Hulk's raw power on the
screen finally mirrors what we've seen in

00:09:40.839 --> 00:09:45.311
the comics, and the visual effects were never
more impressive before what we saw here, with

00:09:45.311 --> 00:09:49.700
the Hulk's face actually looking like a green,
pumped-up version of the actor playing Bruce

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:50.200
Banner.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:55.320
Just like in Ang Lee's film, we're made to
wait quite a bit to finally see the Hulk emerge,

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:59.370
the build-up is perfect and there's enough
room in the movie for him to shine in the

00:09:59.370 --> 00:09:59.860
time given.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:05.280
Unless the impossible happens and Mark Ruffalo
gets his own solo Hulk flick, the Green Goliath

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:08.760
is unlikely to get a better portrayal than
what we saw in Avengers.

